It contributed to the wealth of Stamford, allowing barley to be transported to the town for malting.","title":"Stamford Canal"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Fourteen Locks (grid reference [ ST277886] ) is a series of locks, also known as the Cefn Flight, on the Crumlin arm of the Monmouthshire Canal at Rogerstone in Newport, South Wales.","title":"Fourteen Locks"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The flight of locks was completed in 1799 and raises the water level 160 ft (50 m) in just 800 yd (740 m).","title":"Fourteen Locks"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" This is one of the steepest rises for a major run in the UK which, combined with the sheer number of locks, makes it one of the most significant in the country.","title":"Fourteen Locks"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The run of locks includes a series of embanked ponds, pounds, sluices and weirs to control the water supply, with no set of gates shared between individual locks.","title":"Fourteen Locks"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" It therefore comprises a flight of locks rather than a lock staircase.","title":"Fourteen Locks"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Canal de la Nieppe was part of the Hazebrouck Canals (French: "Canaux d'Hazebrouck" ).","title":"Canal de la Nieppe"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It connected to the River Lys at Thiennes with a second connection to the River Lys at Merville.","title":"Canal de la Nieppe"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It was 23 km with 4 locks.","title":"Canal de la Nieppe"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The other canals composing the Hazebrouck canals are the Canal de Préaven, Canal de la Bourre, Canal d'Hazebrouck.","title":"Canal de la Nieppe"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Wigan Pier is the name given today to the area around the canal at the bottom of the Wigan flight of locks on the Leeds and Liverpool Canal.","title":"Wigan Pier"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is a popular location for visitors and the local community in Wigan, Greater Manchester, England, situated just a few hundred yards south-west of the town centre.","title":"Wigan Pier"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The name "Wigan Pier" has humorous or ironic connotations since it conjures an image of a seaside pleasure pier, whereas Wigan is in fact an inland and traditionally industrial town.","title":"Wigan Pier"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Miami and Erie Canal was a canal in Ohio that ran about 274 mi ; it was constructed from Cincinnati to Toledo to create a water route from the Ohio River to Lake Erie.","title":"Miami and Erie Canal"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Construction on the canal began in 1825 and was completed in 1845 at a cost to the state government of $8,062,680.07.","title":"Miami and Erie Canal"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" At its peak, it included 19 aqueducts, three guard locks, 103 canal locks, multiple feeder canals, and a few man-made water reservoirs.","title":"Miami and Erie Canal"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The canal climbed 395 ft above Lake Erie and 513 ft above the Ohio River to reach a topographical peak called the Loramie Summit, which extended 19 mi between New Bremen, Ohio to lock 1-S in Lockington, north of Piqua, Ohio.","title":"Miami and Erie Canal"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Boats up to 80 feet long were towed along the canal by mules, horses, or oxen walking on a prepared towpath along the bank, at a rate of four to five miles per hour.","title":"Miami and Erie Canal"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Leeds and Liverpool Canal and Monmouthshire Canal
